WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.770
-Welcome to "The Tonight Show."
You're here.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.037
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:07.039
You're watching at home,
thank you.

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.042
Well, guys, everyone was
talking about this.

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:13.146
Today, Facebook and Instagram
were hit by a massive outage

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:16.081
and stopped working for
millions of users.

00:00:16.081 --> 00:00:18.951
Yeah. It was amazing.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.519
[ Audience boos ]

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:23.590
Yeah. Get it out.
Get it out, yeah.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.757
-Wow.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:26.492
-Facebook was
only down for a day,

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:28.661
and in that short time,
everyone got the vaccine.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:29.662
Isn't that amazing?

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.931
Amazing.
Amazing how that happened.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.400
I don't know.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.737
Yes, Facebook's
entire site crashed.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:38.938
They were like, "Oh, my God.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.708
This is the best press we've
had in months!"

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:44.811
Meanwhile, people
who couldn't use Instagram

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:45.979
spent the day posting their

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:48.214
weekend pumpkin-patch
selfies on LinkedIn.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.316
That's not good.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.118
No one cares. You know.

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:53.219
"Isn't he cute?"

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.988
"Well, kind of.
He's 24 now, isn't he?

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:57.790
"Yeah, but he still likes --"

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.625
-"He's still cute."

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:02.128
-Speaking of Facebook,
on "Meet the Press" yesterday,

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.663
a journalist
who has been covering

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.632
the social-media company
was on the show,

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.266
but he was wearing
a very interesting accessory.

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:08.734
Take a look at this.

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:11.203 line:0%
-So, joining for me for more on
all of this is Jeff Horwitz.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:12.372 line:0%
He's one of
"The Wall Street Journal"

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:14.774 line:0%
reporters who's been
reporting on Facebook

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.076 line:0%
under their umbrella
"Facebook Files."

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:18.578
-Yeah.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.680
Chuck Todd was like,
"And right now,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.751
Zooming in from his kid's karate
tournament is Jeff Horwitz.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:25.752
Jeff."

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.186
Well, this is exciting.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:28.487 line:0%
It was just announced that,
next week,

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.224 line:0%
90-year-old legendary actor
William Shatner,

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.493 line:0%
also known as Captain Kirk,
will travel to space

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:37.130 line:0%
aboard Jeff Bezos'
Blue Origin rocket.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.400 line:0%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.901
Yeah.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.572
You can tell Elon Musk
is jealous of Bezos' idea,

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:49.942
'cause he just yelled,
"Get me Betty White on Line 2!

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:53.545
Rose, you're going to space."

00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.781
I love how Elon Musk sounds like
Ralph Kramden now.

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:57.550
-Rose! To the moon!

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.085
-To the moon, Rose!

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:03.556
Meanwhile, I heard about another
new option for space travel --

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.224
I don't know if you saw this --
that's now available.

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:06.292
Watch this. This is real.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.861 line:0%
-Instead of riding a rocket,

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:10.830 line:0%
this is a slow ride to
the edge of space

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.165 line:0%
inside a pressurized capsule
hanging beneath

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:14.867 line:0%
a massive helium balloon.

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:18.270 line:0%
The view will be spectacular
inside a first-class cabin

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.709 line:0%
complete with massive windows,
Wi-Fi, even a bar and restroom.

00:02:25.444 --> 00:02:27.714
-A helium balloon, yeah.

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.348
Sounds all well and good
until there is a leak,

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:30.416
and you're like...

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.617
[ High-pitched ]
"Okay, I'm freaking out.

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:33.519
Mayday, mayday!
Houston, we have a problem!

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.421
Please -- No, no!

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:38.391
Alvin! Alvin!"

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:45.498
[ Normal voice ] I actually hope
they send Shatner

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:46.732
on the balloon so
it will feel like

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:48.401
a live-action remake of "Up."

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:51.304
-Oh!
-Yeah.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.172
I think that'd be cool.

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:54.506
Some sports news.

00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:57.409
Last night was Tom Brady's big
return to New England.

00:02:57.409 --> 00:02:59.078
Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.246
Great game.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:02.648
Brady faced his old coach,
Bill Belichick.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:04.317
And you can tell
the two men still have

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:05.551
a lot of love for each other.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.620
Check out how long
they hugged after the game.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.787 line:0%
-Good game.

00:03:08.787 --> 00:03:10.924 line:0%
-Well, well, there it is. Okay.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.830 line:0%
-Can we throw a timer
on that hug?

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.232 line:0%
-Well, well, there it is. Okay.

00:03:20.232 --> 00:03:23.736
-Wow. I've had longer hugs
with strangers on the bus.

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.104
It's like...

00:03:27.172 --> 00:03:28.341
Well, this is interesting.

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.077
The future royalties from
the Folgers jingle,

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.247
"The Best Part of Wakin' Up"
are being sold at auction.

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.882
-Ah! Correction, Jimmy.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.684
They were being sold at auction.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:41.287
The Roots all pooled
our money together

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:44.190
and snapped that jingle up
immediately.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.526
And now we just wait
for the money to roll in.

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:51.396
-Whoa! You guys own
the Folgers jingle?

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:54.733
-♪ The best part of buying it ♪

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:58.938
♪ Is getting paid to say
these words ♪

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.974
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:07.613
-How much money
do you get for that?

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:10.616
-♪ We just made 11 cents ♪

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:16.389
♪ Now we made 11 more ♪

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.792
-Yeah. Hmm.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:22.161
It doesn't seem like very much.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:25.664
-♪ The key is to sing
this tune ♪

00:04:25.664 --> 00:04:30.169
♪ To everything we say ♪

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.937
-Gotcha, gotcha.

00:04:31.937 --> 00:04:35.908
So, how much did you have to bid
to win the royalties?

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:39.545
-♪ We dropped 60,000 bucks ♪

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:43.349
♪ Plus tickets to our show ♪

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:47.854
-Oh. Let's see. $60,000.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:52.157
Okay, so in order to break even,
you need to sing that song --

00:04:52.157 --> 00:04:55.762
-♪ 5,450 times ♪

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.996
♪ Followed by four more ♪

00:04:57.996 --> 00:04:59.165
-Alright, yeah, guys,

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:02.568
this doesn't seem like
a very smart investment.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:06.372
-♪ Regret it deeply
to our core ♪

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:10.442
♪ It was a huge mistake ♪

00:05:10.442 --> 00:05:12.245
-Well...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:17.015 --> 00:05:19.518
Well, in that case...

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:21.987
♪ I'm glad I could
help you out ♪

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:25.057
♪ Now let's move on
with the show ♪

00:05:25.057 --> 00:05:29.395
-That will be --
that will be 11 cents, Jimmy.

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:33.132
-[ Chuckles ]
You're not serious, right?

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:36.702
-♪ Pay the 11 cents ♪

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:42.041
♪ Or we'll see you in court,
mother[bleep] ♪

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.777
-Are you kidding me?

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.779
-That is rude!

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:48.113
-That's the original lyrics
to the jingle?

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:49.982
-That's the original lyrics
to the song?

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.084
-We just got fined $60,000.
-Oh, my God.

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:53.585
-They'll never make
that money back.

00:05:53.585 --> 00:05:55.621
-I guess there's more than
Folgers in your cup.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.690
-Yeah.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.424 line:0%
Hey, everyone,
I saw that two scientists

00:05:59.424 --> 00:06:01.961 line:0%
were just awarded the
Nobel Prize in medicine

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:03.429 line:0%
for discovering the receptors

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:06.599 line:0%
that let humans feel
temperature and touch.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.001
Meanwhile, the scientists who
saved millions of lives

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:12.872
by creating the COVID vaccine
were like, "Yeah."

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.640
That deserves a Nobel Prize.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.177
The stove's hot.
Way to go, guys. Yeah."

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.211
But get this.

00:06:19.211 --> 00:06:21.147
Their research
did find one human

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:23.282
without the capacity to feel.

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:24.483
Here he is.

00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:27.853 line:0%
-Good game.
-Well, well, there it is. Okay.

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:30.423
-Ice. Ice.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.203
20 years they worked together.
Yeah.

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:46.038
Some entertainment news.
This is amazing.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:48.140
"Squid Game" is now on track

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.743
to be Netflix's
most-watched show ever.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.445
They will be on
our show this week.

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:54.213
-Whoa!
-That's right.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:56.648
It's a terrifying
dystopian drama.

00:06:56.648 --> 00:06:59.418
Now other TV networks
want to get in on the action,

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.654
and I already saw an ad
for a spin-off.

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:02.688
Watch this.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:05.090
-From the creators of
"Squid Game"

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.559
comes a new
and horrifying series

00:07:07.559 --> 00:07:09.328
on Nickelodeon.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:12.331
"Squidward Game."

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:14.533 line:0%
-Whoa! I've never played
this game before.

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:15.534 line:0%
What's it called?

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.336
-It's called...

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:21.107
[ Distorted ]
"Sign in blood or Patrick dies!"

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:22.908
-"Squidward Game."

00:07:22.908 --> 00:07:26.545
-I can't do it.
I can't do it, Squidward.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:27.980
-Catch it after "Paw Patrol."

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:31.017
-Yeah, it's on after.
It's on after "Paw Patrol."

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:32.718
You get that out of the way.

00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:37.489
And, finally, an Ohio judge
ruled that a man

00:07:37.489 --> 00:07:41.359
wasn't allowed to sue his wife
after he tripped over her shoes

00:07:41.359 --> 00:07:43.495
and fell down the stairs.

00:07:43.495 --> 00:07:46.198
On the bright side,
the judge did rule in favor of

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:49.068
putting a camera in their car
for the ride home from court.

